= Concept — Technical =

== Competing ontologies ==
Major theories identify concepts with:

* mental representations;
* cognitive abilities;
* abstract objects.

These positions differ about individuation, compositionality, public shareability and psychological realization.

== Prototype / exemplar / theory views ==
Prototype theories represent categories using central tendencies or typical features.

Exemplar theories classify by comparison with stored instances.

Theory-based views embed concepts in causal/explanatory knowledge.

Hybrid accounts combine several mechanisms.

== Compositionality ==
Structured thought requires some way to combine concepts.

If complex concepts are formed compositionally, their content depends on component concepts plus combination rules.

Natural-language concept composition remains difficult because prototype effects and context can change expected meanings.

== SKOS semantics ==
SKOS gives concepts URIs and relationships such as:

<pre>
skos:broader
skos:narrower
skos:related
skos:exactMatch
skos:closeMatch
</pre>

These are designed for knowledge organization, not for asserting full OWL class subsumption.

In particular, broader/narrower links should not automatically be read as formal subclass relations.

== Concept / class separation ==
A formal class can be interpreted extensionally as a set of individuals.

A concept may instead be:
* intensional;
* psychologically represented;
* intentionally vague;
* context-sensitive;
* non-extensional.

Conflating the two can create false logical consequences.

